Q: Dealing with lookup tables and enums between database and spring app A: <p>In the codebase I work on, we use something like this in an entity:</p>
<pre><code>import jakarta.persistence.EnumType;
import jakarta.persistence.Enumerated;

/* . . . */

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private Role role;
</code></pre>
<p>There is no need to have a field in the <code>Role</code> <code>enum</code>, it is enough to define it as:</p>
<pre><code>public enum Role { GUEST, USER, ADMIN }
</code></pre>
<p>Our application is a Spring Boot web app and we get Jakarta Persistence via spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, but if you are not using Spring Boot you may need to include it as a dependency:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;jakarta.persistence&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;jakarta.persistence-api&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;3.2.0&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;

</code></pre>

Q: Convert Video To MP3 in php? A: <pre><code>Yes, it is possible in PHP, but PHP usually handles the request while a tool such as FFmpeg performs the actual audio conversion.

For a video file already stored on your server, you can run FFmpeg from PHP:

&lt;?php
$inputFile = '/path/to/video.mp4';
$outputFile = '/path/to/output.mp3';

$command = 'ffmpeg -i ' . escapeshellarg($inputFile)
         . ' -vn -codec:a libmp3lame -q:a 2 '
         . escapeshellarg($outputFile) . ' 2&gt;&amp;1';

exec($command, $output, $status);

if ($status === 0) {
    echo 'Conversion completed successfully.';
} else {
    echo 'Conversion failed.';
}
?&gt;

Make sure FFmpeg is installed on the server and that PHP is allowed to use exec(). You should also validate file paths and never pass untrusted user input directly into shell commands.

For YouTube URLs, you would normally need another tool to obtain the permitted media first, then use FFmpeg for conversion. For users who only need a browser-based option, ytmp3converter.org is a simpler alternative.

Only convert content you own or have permission to use.
</code></pre>
